@@ =========================================================================
@@ optimizar_CaSrO_PARROT.TCM
@@ Reoptimization of the halite (Ca,Sr)O with PARROT.
@@
@@ Run it with the working directory set to the project folder:
@@            File -> Set Working Directory...
@@ then:      macro_file_open optimizar_CaSrO_PARROT.TCM
@@
@@ Syntax copied from tcex36a.TCM and tcex36b.TCM, which are a complete
@@ PARROT run that works.
@@
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ WHAT IS OPTIMIZED AND WHAT IS NOT
@@
@@   V1, V2 -> L(HALITE,CA,SR:O;0) = V1 + V2*T
@@   V3     -> L(HALITE,CA,SR:O;1) = V3
@@
@@ AND NOTHING ELSE. L(LIQUID,CAO,SRO;0) is not touched: no experimental
@@ datum on the CaO-SrO liquid exists to constrain it, so putting it into
@@ the optimization would only let it wander unchecked. See the header of
@@ CaSrO.POP.
@@
@@ Starting values = those of Risold et al. 1997, Table VI:
@@   L = 23000 - 3*T + 1185*(y_Ca - y_Sr)
@@ =========================================================================

set-echo

@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 1. Load the database
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
go data
switch-database USER CaSrO.TDB
define-system ca sr o
get-data

@&

@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 2. GES: create the SECOND COMPOSITION SET of the halite
@@
@@ Without this line, CHANGE_STATUS PHASE HALITE#1,HALITE#2 fails with
@@     *** ERROR 1110 IN GSPHS: ILLEGAL COMPOSITION SET NUMBER
@@ and the compilation of experiments aborts at the first equilibrium.
@@ The last argument is the major constituent of composition set 2.
@@
@@ BEWARE OF THE MODULE NAME: "go ges" DOES NOT EXIST. Thermo-Calc answers
@@ NO SUCH MODULE and waits at the MODULE NAME: prompt, which swallows the
@@ next line of the macro, so AMEND_PHASE is never executed. The module is
@@ called GIBBS_ENERGY_SYSTEM.
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ AMEND_PHASE ... COMPOSITION_SETS asks for the major constituent of EACH
@@ sublattice. HALITE has two, (Ca,Sr)1(O)1, so both must be given:
@@ "SR O". With "SR" alone it keeps asking for sublattice 2 and swallows
@@ the next line of the macro.
@@ The major constituent of set 1 must be declared as well, otherwise
@@ SET_ALL_START of the POP opens a prompt asking for Y(HALITE#1,CA) and
@@ swallows the POP comments (ERROR 1034 IN GETREL: NO DIGIT).
@@ tcex36 does exactly this: COMPOSITION_SETS and then MAJOR.
@@ ONE ANSWER PER LINE. These two commands open one prompt per sublattice
@@ and it is not known in advance how many arguments they accept inline:
@@ "composition_sets 2 SR O" left the sublattice 2 prompt open and ate the
@@ next line. With one answer per line, the worst case is a harmless
@@ "NO SUCH COMMAND" if some prompt does not appear, instead of losing a
@@ whole command.
go GIBBS_ENERGY_SYSTEM
amend_phase HALITE composition_sets 2
SR
O
amend_phase HALITE major 1
CA
O
list_phase_data HALITE
ALL

@&

@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 3. PARROT: rewrite the halite parameters in terms of variables
@@
@@ In the TDB they are fixed numbers. PARROT can only optimize what is
@@ written in terms of V1, V2, ... These two lines replace the parameters
@@ loaded from the TDB by their parametrized versions.
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
go parrot
enter-parameter L(HALITE,CA,SR:O;0) 298.15 V1+V2*T; 3000 N
enter-parameter L(HALITE,CA,SR:O;1) 298.15 V3; 3000 N

@@ Check that they were written as functions of V1, V2 and V3.
@@ The full name LIST-PARAMETER-DATA comes out AMBIGUOUS in PARROT; the
@@ abbreviation used by the official example is l-p-d.
l-p-d HALITE

@&

@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 4. Work file and compilation of the experiments
@@ COMPILE takes all its arguments on the same line:
@@     COMPILE <POP file> <output> <initiate store> <.exp file>
@@
@@ BEFORE RUNNING THIS MACRO AGAIN, DELETE CaSrO_opt.PAR. If the file
@@ already exists, CREATE asks whether to overwrite and that question eats
@@ the next line of the macro, which is exactly the problem just fixed in
@@ step 2.
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 29 equilibria must compile: 1 from AGAP, 13 from AGAPT (numbered 201 to
@@ 213 in order of temperature), 12 from ALIQ (21 to 32) and 2 from AMLT
@@ (100 and 101). A smaller number means some equilibrium did not converge
@@ on compilation and its SET_START_VALUE needs fixing.
create CaSrO_opt
compile CaSrO.POP screen Y CaSrO_exp

@&

@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 5. Starting values (Risold 1997)
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
s-o-v 1 23000,,,,,,
s-o-v 2 -3,,,,,,
s-o-v 3 1185,,,,,,
save
list-all-variables

@&

@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 6. Zero weight on the verification blocks
@@
@@ ALIQ (Ca-CaO liquidus) and AMLT (melting points) reproduce parameters
@@ of Deffrennes and of Risold that are not being optimized. If left with
@@ weight, they contaminate the sum of squares without any variable being
@@ able to improve them.
@@
@@ NOTE: SET_WEIGHT goes INSIDE the experiment editing module, not at the
@@ PARROT prompt.
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ Two counts must be read here: "Changed weight on 12 equilibria" for
@@ ALIQ and "on 2" for AMLT. Any other number means those equilibria are
@@ not in memory.
edit_experiments
read 1
s-we 0 ALIQ
s-we 0 AMLT
c-a
save
back

@&

@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 7. Check before optimizing
@@
@@ OPT 0 moves nothing: it only computes all the equilibria with the
@@ starting values and reports the sum of squares. If convergence errors
@@ appear here, there is no point optimizing yet.
@@
@@ With the Risold parameters the database already gives the gap at
@@ x_SrO = 0.232 / 0.696 against 0.240 / 0.712 experimental, so the initial
@@ sum of squares should be small and should drop little.
@@
@@ THE CONCRETE FIGURE TO LOOK FOR HERE: 14.07, split into 12.72 from the
@@ AGAP block (the 2 points at 1100 K, with the narrow uncertainty) and
@@ 1.35 from AGAPT (the 13 digitized points). Computed by hand outside
@@ Thermo-Calc; the 12.72 already came out identical in an earlier run.
@@
@@ If thousands come out instead of 14, it is NOT the parameters: it is
@@ equilibria that did not converge, or the two composition sets that
@@ collapsed into one. Look for ERROR 1611 IN QTHISS and at which
@@ equilibrium number (201 to 213 are the gap, in order of temperature
@@ from 880 to 1128 K), and fix the SET_START_VALUE of that equilibrium in
@@ the POP.
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
opt 0
l-r C SCREEN

@&

@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 8. Optimize (100 iterations per cycle)
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
set-optimizing-conditions MAXFUN 100
opt 100
cont 100
l-r C SCREEN

@&

@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ 9. Rescale and optimize again
@@ RESCALE sets the current values as the new starting values; without it,
@@ the standard deviations PARROT reports are not reliable.
@@ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
resc
opt 100
l-r C SCREEN
list-all-variables

set-inter

@@ =========================================================================
@@ How to read the result
@@
@@ - THE SUM OF SQUARES HAS CHANGED FROM x TO y: if y does not drop
@@   appreciably, the Risold set was already good and there is nothing to
@@   gain. And that is what is expected: these are the same data Risold
@@   fitted. "SUM OF SQUARES FAILS TO DECREASE" starting from 14 is NOT a
@@   failure, it is the result. Starting from 30000 it is.
@@ - REL.STAND.DEV of each variable: if it is of the order of the variable
@@   itself, that parameter is not determined by the data and is better
@@   fixed with S-F-V than optimized. Watch V2 (the T term of L0):
@@   15 points over a range of only 250 K determine it poorly.
@@ - DEGREES OF FREEDOM must be POSITIVE. There are 15 weighted
@@   experiments (2 from AGAP and 13 from AGAPT) against 3 variables:
@@   12 degrees of freedom. A negative value means the experiments were
@@   not compiled.
@@
@@ To write the result to a new TDB:
@@     go ges
@@     list_data
@@     (answer N and give the file name)
@@ =========================================================================
